RootView.swift:83-86 is the toast's auto-clear:

    .task {
        try? await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: 4_000_000_000)
        reactionService.clearIncoming()
    }

`try?` swallows the CancellationError that Task.sleep throws, and clearIncoming() then runs UNCONDITIONALLY — a cancelled timer clears the toast immediately instead of not clearing it. Round 1 had exactly one instance of this view, on the base overlay (old RootView.swift:135-160, single `.overlay(alignment:.top)`), attached to a ZStack that is never torn down, so the cancellation branch was never taken. Round 2 extracted the toast into the `reactionToast` computed property (:63-88) and instantiated it a SECOND time at :158, as an overlay on `coverContent(for:)` — a view whose lifetime is the cover's. SwiftUI cancels a `.task` when its view is removed, so the cover instance's timer is now cancelled on every cover teardown, and cancellation clears the toast.

Trace: the sender is in the camera cover (destination == .camera). A reaction lands (ReactionService.swift:198-199 sets incoming + incomingDropTitle). Both toast instances appear; both 4 s timers start. At t=1.2 s the sender taps the escape chevron (ARScreen.swift:189-192 → router.showCamera=false → AppRouter.swift:78-83 destination=nil). The cover dismisses, the cover's overlay subtree is removed, its `.task` is cancelled, `try? await` returns at once and `reactionService.clearIncoming()` executes — incoming goes nil at t≈1.2 s and the base copy vanishes in the same frame the cover slides away. The same fires on camera→memory and memory→create (item-identity changes on `fullScreenCover(item:)` are a documented dismiss-and-replace) and on CreateWizard's dismiss.

A second, smaller defect in the same task: it carries no `id:`, so when `incoming` changes A→B the `if let` branch identity is unchanged and the timer does NOT restart — a second reaction inherits the first's remaining window (B arriving at t=3.8 s is cleared at t=4.0 s). And with two timers on independent clocks, the copy inside a cover is always cleared by whichever timer started first, so its visible life is ≤4 s from the reaction's ARRIVAL, not from cover entry.

HomeScreen.swift:146-153, added by round 2:

    private func forYouCard(_ drop: GeoDrop) -> some View {
        Button {
            reactionSession.startWatching(drop)
            router.showCamera = true
        } label: { ... }

Nothing on the path is conditioned on proximity or on the drop ever being rendered. `receivedDrops` (HomeScreen.swift:53-57) filters only on `receivedViaLink == true` and sorts by distance — there is no radius filter — and the card's own subline (:179-185) renders '3.1 km away · tap when you're close' for exactly the far case while remaining a single tappable Button covering that text.

Full chain from one tap at 3 km: startWatching (ReactionSession.swift:29-37) sets phase=.watching and watchStartedAt=now; ARScreen.swift:26-30 renders ReactionOverlay because reactionSession.drop != nil; ReactionOverlay (ReactionSession.swift:124-138) shows 'LIVE · <sender> sees your reactions' over an empty viewfinder; the emoji bar (:179-215) is live, and a tap issues a REAL Firestore write via ReactionService.send (ReactionService.swift:67-92) that toasts the sender, plus didReact→startWrap(15) (:55-63). Fifteen seconds later phase=.memory; RootView.swift:165-172 sees destination==.camera and presents MemoryScreen, which renders 'That made <their> day.' (MemoryScreen.swift:48), the polaroid (:111-150) of an empty room, '❤️ Your reaction reached <sender>' (:181-188) and '👀 Watched live 15 S' (:87). Round 1's card did none of this — it only opened the camera — so the whole chain is newly reachable.
